export const lpContent={
 'a-apple':{
  title:'Aa — Apple',one:'The very first letter sound — the wide-open mouth sound that starts apple.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Aa Lesson: Teach the A Sound with Apple (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Aa and its short /a/ sound, taught with the apple card — canvas tracing, a doctor game and an A-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson opens the whole alphabet: the letter Aa and its first sound, the big open "ah" that starts apple. Your child traces the letter on the card, plays the doctor game that makes the sound naturally, and hunts A-sounds around the home.',
  what:'Aa is the letter that stands for the first sound in apple — a short, wide "ah". It is the first letter of the alphabet and the first sound on the path to reading.',
  mean:'The big idea hiding in this lesson: a letter is a picture of a sound. When your child sees Aa and hears "ah" in their head, reading has officially started.',
  know:'Your child needs three things from this card: that Aa stands for the apple sound, that the sound is short and open (not the letter name "ay"), and that the sound can start many words, not only apple.',
  why:'/a/ is one of the most common first sounds in little-word land — apple, ant, animal, am. A solid "ah" makes every later vowel lesson easier, because the mouth already knows how to open wide.',
  dev:'Wide-open mouth control (the biggest mouth-shape a vowel asks for), sound-to-letter mapping, and the listening habit of catching a sound at the start of a word.',
  vocab:'a, apple, ant, apron, alligator, ah',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Aa. This is the letter. It says ah — ah — apple."\n2) Finger-trace the big Aa on the canvas while saying "ah" the whole way round — eyes, fingertip and voice on the same letter at once.\n3) Play the doctor game below: the sound arrives on its own, no drilling needed.\n4) Hunt three a-things in the room and name each one with a big open mouth: apple, animal, apron.\n5) Finish by letting your child say "ah — apple" all by themselves, and celebrate loudly.',
  say:'"Open your mouth like the doctor asks — ahhh. That is the sound! A says ahh, like the start of apple. Say it with me and let your mouth open as wide as it can."',
  sayhow:'Drop the jaw, tongue flat and low, and let the voice out with nothing blocking it: "ah". No lip rounding, no teeth touching — the sound is pure open mouth.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Aa sits beside a shiny red apple with a single green leaf, and the word "apple" is printed underneath — letter, picture and word in one glance.',
  ask:'"What sound does A make?" "What is the picture word on this card?" "Can you find something that starts with ah?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "A says ah!" "Ah — apple!" or, mid-doctor-game, a very serious "ahhh" with the tongue out like a real check-up.',
  real:'The doctor game is the secret: every check-up ends with "say ahh" — and that IS the A sound. Add apple slices at snack time and ants on the pavement, and the sound has three homes already.',
  play:'Doctor check-up: wrap a scarf around your head, hold a spoon like a tongue depressor and ask your patient to "say ahh". After three "ahhhs", the doctor prescribes an a-word — apple, ant, apron. Swap roles and be the patient yourself.',
  check:'Show the card again tomorrow and ask "what sound?" — a quick "ah!" (not "ay") means the sound has landed. If they give the letter name, replay the doctor game; the name can wait for the alphabet song.',
  tips:'The sound and the name are two different lessons. If "ay" keeps arriving, go back to the open-mouth game — the mouth remembers what the ears forget, and apples help.'},
 'b-ball':{
  title:'Bb — Ball',one:'The lip-pop sound with the voice buzzing, anchored to every child\u2019s first favourite toy.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Bb Lesson: Teach the B Sound with Ball (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Bb and its buzzing /b/ sound, taught with the ball card — canvas tracing, a balloon game and a B-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Bb and its lip-pop sound with the ball card: your child traces the letter, blows up an imaginary balloon one b-word at a time, and learns that b is a sound they can feel on their own lips.',
  what:'Bb is the letter that stands for the first sound in ball — a quick pop made with two lips and a buzzing voice.',
  mean:'B teaches that sounds live in the body, not just in the ear: /b/ happens on the lips, so your child can watch themselves say it in a mirror — a sound you can see is a sound you remember.',
  know:'Your child needs to feel the lips press together before the pop, hear the voice buzzing behind the pop, and carry the sound onto other words — ball, bat, bubble — not just the picture word.',
  why:'/b/ opens dozens of everyday words — ball, bath, book, breakfast, bus. It is also the twin of /p/, and children who feel the buzz-versus-puff difference early never mix the two spellings later.',
  dev:'Lip strength and control, voice awareness (feeling the throat buzz), and mirror self-checking — the first step toward fixing sounds without being told.',
  vocab:'b, ball, bat, bubble, bath, big',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Bb. Press your lips together — b! It says the ball sound."\n2) Finger-trace the tall Bb on the canvas while humming "b-b-b" — the tracing finger and the buzzing lips work together.\n3) Play the balloon game below so the sound gets a body to live in.\n4) Hunt b-things: a book, a bottle, a bear. Say each one with the same lip pop.\n5) End with the mirror check: lips together, voice on — "that is a real b!"',
  say:'"Press your lips together like this — now hum until they pop open: buh. That is b! B says buh, like ball. Bounce the sound: buh, buh, ball."',
  sayhow:'Lips sealed, then released with the voice already running: "buh". The throat buzzes the whole time — a hand on the throat proves it. Keep the pop short; no "buh-uh" drag.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Bb sits beside a smiling beach ball in yellow, red and blue, with the word "ball" printed below — the toy most children already own, wearing its first letter.',
  ask:'"Where do your lips go for b?" "Does your throat buzz when you say it?" "What b-word should we bounce next?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "B! buh-buh-ball!" or — halfway through the balloon game — the classic complaint: "My lips are tired." Tired lips are learning lips.',
  real:'Ball games, bath time and bubble mix are three b-factories you already own. On walks, every bus, bench and bird is a b-spot waiting to be claimed out loud.',
  play:'The balloon game: mime blowing up a balloon — one b-word per breath, and the balloon grows bigger each time: ball… ball-bat… ball-bat-bubble. On the biggest word, let go: the balloon flies around the room with a long "bbbbbb" until it lands.',
  check:'Ask for the sound, never the letter: "what sound does B make?" — a clean "buh" with lips visibly pressed is the goal. Then swap in a picture of a dog: if they say "nope, that is d", the sounds are sorted.',
  tips:'If b comes out as p, slow everything down and add the throat hand: b buzzes, p does not. Never drill longer than five minutes — the lips learn fast and sulk when tired.'},
 'c-cat':{
  title:'Cc — Cat',one:'The clicking sound from the back of the mouth, anchored to the classic first pet word.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Cc Lesson: Teach the C Sound with Cat (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Cc and its /k/ click, taught with the cat card — canvas tracing, a castle-knock game and a C-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Cc and its click sound with the cat card: your child traces the letter, knocks on an imaginary castle door one c-word at a time, and discovers that the sound lives at the very back of the mouth.',
  what:'Cc is the letter that stands for the first sound in cat — a clean, short /k/ click made when the back of the tongue lets go of the roof of the mouth.',
  mean:'C introduces a listening challenge: the click happens where no eye can see it. So this lesson teaches ears-first learning — the same skill every later sound lesson will reuse.',
  know:'Your child needs to hear the click as a tiny door shutting, say it cleanly (no "kuh-uh" drag), and collect a small family of c-words — cat, cup, car — so the sound detaches from one picture.',
  why:'/c/ is everywhere in a child\u2019s day — cup, coat, car, cake, colour. It is also the first letter with a hidden second job (soft c, as in circus), and knowing the hard click first keeps that later surprise small.',
  dev:'Back-of-mouth tongue control, careful listening (the click is invisible), and word-family building — one sound, many words.',
  vocab:'c, cat, cup, car, coat, click',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Cc. It says the cat sound — a little click at the back: c!"\n2) Finger-trace the Cc on the canvas — the open curve matches the open back of the mouth.\n3) Play the castle-knock game below; the click IS the knock.\n4) Collect c-things: a cup, a carrot, a coat. Say each with one clean click.\n5) Finish with three fast clicks — c, c, cat — and a victory meow.',
  say:'"Knock knock — who is at the door? C! C says k, a tiny click like a door shutting: cat. Say it like a secret knock: c, c, cat."',
  sayhow:'Open the mouth slightly, lift the back of the tongue and let it drop: "k". Keep it dry and short — one click, no vowel hanging on. If "kuh-uh" appears, say it like a whisper-tick.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Cc sits beside a smiling orange tabby cat, with the word "cat" printed underneath — the most classic of all first phonics words.',
  ask:'"Can you feel the click at the back of your mouth?" "What did the cat say at the door?" "Name a c-word in this room."',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "c-c-c-cat!" with three knocks, or invent the best game of all: knocking on the actual table for every c-word they can find.',
  real:'The kitchen is a c-museum: cups, cabinets, carrots, cake tins. Car rides supply car, seat, clunk-click. And every story with a cat in it re-opens the door for one more knock.',
  play:'Castle knock: build a pillow fortress, and the only way in is the password — a c-word said with a proper click. Knock knock: "cat!" — the gate opens. Inside, the royal cat demands three more c-words before bed.',
  check:'Ask "what sound does C make?" and listen for a clean click, not the letter name "see". Then test the family: cup, car, coat — three clicks in a row means the sound travels.',
  tips:'Do not mention soft c (circus, celery) yet — one job per card. And if c and k blur together, say honestly: "they are twins, same sound, two coats" — the honesty saves tears later.'},
 'd-dog':{
  title:'Dd — Dog',one:'The tongue-tap sound with the voice on, anchored to the animal half of children answer questions with.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Dd Lesson: Teach the D Sound with Dog (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Dd and its tapping /d/ sound, taught with the dog card — canvas tracing, a doorbell game and a D-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Dd and its tongue-tap sound with the dog card: your child traces the letter, plays doorbell with an imaginary front door, and feels exactly where the tongue lands for every d-word.',
  what:'Dd is the letter that stands for the first sound in dog — a quick tap of the tongue tip just behind the top teeth, with the voice switched on.',
  mean:'D teaches that some sounds have an address: the tongue taps one exact spot, every time. Finding that spot is body knowledge — and body knowledge never fades.',
  know:'Your child needs to feel the tap behind the teeth, keep the voice buzzing through it, and let d start a whole visitor list of words — dog, dad, duck, door.',
  why:'/d/ starts the words that run a family\u2019s day: dad, dinner, door, done. It is the voiced twin of /t/, and feeling the buzz difference now is cheaper than fixing the spelling confusion later.',
  dev:'Tongue-tip precision, voice-on/voice-off awareness, and the rhythm habit — one tap, one sound, one word.',
  vocab:'d, dog, dad, duck, door, drum',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Dd. The tongue taps — d! It says the dog sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Dd on the canvas while tapping: one curve of the letter, one tap of the tongue.\n3) Play the doorbell game below — every visitor brings a d-word.\n4) Tap out three d-things you can see or pretend: dog, doll, doughnut.\n5) Finish with the drum finale: four fast taps, one loud "d-d-d-dog!"',
  say:'"Put your tongue behind your teeth and let it bounce once — duh. That is d! D says duh, like dog. Knock the sound: d, d, dog."',
  sayhow:'Tongue tip up to the ridge behind the top teeth, then release with the voice on: "duh". Quick and light — the tap is one frame long, not a held sound.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Dd sits beside a smiling golden puppy wearing a green collar, with the word "dog" printed below — the animal most children name first, now wearing its letter.',
  ask:'"Where does your tongue go for d?" "Does your voice buzz?" "Who should come to our door next — a duck or a doll?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "d-d-d-dog!" like a drumroll, or — best case — start answering every "knock knock" at home with a new d-word.',
  real:'Every front door in your life is a d-game now: knock, say a d-word, enter. Walks supply dogs (real ones bark along), dads, and delivery vans that go ding-dong.',
  play:'Doorbell game: draw a door on paper or use a real door. Each "ding-dong" means a visitor arrives — and the visitor must be a d-word: dog, duck, dad, dragon. Open the door, greet the visitor by name, close the door, ring again. Ten rings, ten visitors, zero boredom.',
  check:'Tomorrow, ask "what sound does D make?" and watch the tongue: a visible tap behind the teeth with a buzzing throat is a pass. Then try "t" — if the tap stays but the buzz stops, both twins are learned.',
  tips:'If dog comes out as "tog", the voice switch is off — hand on the throat, exaggerate the buzz. Keep sessions short: the tongue is a muscle and learns best in little bursts.'},
 'e-elephant':{
  title:'Ee — Elephant',one:'The relaxed middle-mouth vowel, anchored to the biggest animal children love to say.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Ee Lesson: Teach the E Sound with Elephant (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Ee and its short /e/ sound, taught with the elephant card — canvas tracing, an egg-crack game and an E-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Ee and its short "eh" sound with the elephant card: your child traces the letter, cracks imaginary eggs that hatch e-words, and learns the relaxed middle-mouth vowel the whole alphabet leans on.',
  what:'Ee is the letter that stands for the first sound in elephant — a short, relaxed "eh" made with the mouth half open and the tongue resting flat.',
  mean:'E is the alphabet\u2019s hardest worker: it appears in more words than almost any other letter. Meeting it as a sound (not just a letter name "ee") saves years of reading guesswork.',
  know:'Your child needs to hear the short "eh" as its own sound — different from "ee" in tree and from "eye" — and say it in a handful of words: elephant, egg, elf.',
  why:'Short /e/ sits inside bed, red, ten, get — words a four-year-old uses daily. It is also the vowel children confuse with short /i/ most often, so an early, well-modelled "eh" pays off for years.',
  dev:'Mid-mouth vowel control, sound isolation in long words (eh-le-phant), and the patience to let a long word start slowly.',
  vocab:'e, elephant, egg, elf, elf, elbow',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Ee. It says eh — the elephant sound. Say it softly: eh."\n2) Finger-trace the Ee on the canvas — three straight arms, like the letter itself.\n3) Play the egg-crack game below: every crack hatches an e-word.\n4) Stretch the long word together: "eh — eh — eh-le-phant". The sound lives at the front.\n5) Finish with three soft "eh"s and one elephant stomp.',
  say:'"Relax your mouth — halfway open, nothing stretched: eh. That is E\u2019s sound, the elephant one. Say it like a tiny echo: eh, eh, elephant."',
  sayhow:'Mouth half open, tongue flat and forward, voice on: "eh". The sound is short and dry — no glide into "ee". If it slides, bite an imaginary lemon and try again.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Ee sits beside a smiling grey elephant with the word "elephant" printed below in its own rounded box — a long, friendly word that starts with the shortest of sounds.',
  ask:'"What sound starts elephant?" "Is your mouth big open, half open or closed for eh?" "What hatches from an egg?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "eh-eh-elephant!" like a trumpet warming up, or announce breakfast facts: "eggs start with eh!" — which is exactly the point.',
  real:'Breakfast is an e-lesson: eggs, elbows on the table. Every elephant in every picture book gets one more "eh". And elbows, escalators and elevators all start with the same relaxed sound.',
  play:'Egg-crack game: tap an imaginary egg on the edge of an imaginary bowl — "eh, eh, CRACK!" — and something e-shaped hatches out: an elf, an elephant, an empty cup. Name the hatchling, crack the next egg. Whoever cracks the most eggs names the most e-things.',
  check:'A day later, show the card and ask for the sound. "Eh!" is the goal; "ee" means the letter name crept in — replay the egg game, and keep your own "eh" short and flat so there is one model only.',
  tips:'Short /e/ and short /i/ mix up for years — that is normal, not failure. Model clearly, accept close attempts, and let many e-words do the sorting rather than corrections.'},
 'f-fish':{
  title:'Ff — Fish',one:'The windy teeth-on-lip sound with no voice, anchored to the animal that swims through bathtime.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Ff Lesson: Teach the F Sound with Fish (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Ff and its windy /f/ sound, taught with the fish card — canvas tracing, a paper-fan game and an F-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Ff and its windy sound with the fish card: your child traces the letter, powers a paper fan with f-breaths, and learns the one sound they can watch move a real object across a table.',
  what:'Ff is the letter that stands for the first sound in fish — air hissing gently between top teeth and bottom lip, with no voice at all.',
  mean:'F is the first "quiet" sound in the set: the throat stays silent while the mouth makes wind. That contrast — voiced versus breath — is a listening skill the whole alphabet will reuse.',
  know:'Your child needs the teeth-on-lip shape, the feel of moving air (not voice), and a small fleet of f-words — fish, frog, fork, fan — so the sound travels beyond one picture.',
  why:'/f/ starts four, five, fish, family — words that organise a child\u2019s whole day. It is also the whisper twin of /v/, and the hand-on-throat check learned here sorts both letters forever.',
  dev:'Lip-and-teeth coordination, air control (blowing gently on purpose), and voice-off awareness — noticing that some sounds run on breath alone.',
  vocab:'f, fish, frog, fork, fan, four',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Ff. Top teeth kiss the bottom lip and blow — f! The fish sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Ff on the canvas: two arms and a leg, while the lips hold the wind.\n3) Play the paper-fan game below — the breath becomes visible and useful.\n4) Collect f-things: a fork, a feather, a picture of a frog. Say each with one soft gust.\n5) Finish with the mirror check: teeth visible on the lip means a true f.',
  say:'"Bite your bottom lip softly with your top teeth — now blow: fff. That is f! F says fff, like fish. Blow the sound: fff, fff, fish."',
  sayhow:'Top teeth rest lightly on the bottom lip, air pushes through, voice off: "fff". It can stretch as long as the breath lasts — the only limit is air.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Ff sits beside a smiling teal fish with the word "fish" printed below — a swims-along animal for a swims-along sound.',
  ask:'"Can you feel the wind on your lip?" "Is your throat quiet or buzzing?" "How long can your f last — three seconds? Five?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "fffish!" with a proper gust, or discover the party trick: f-ing at a candle flame and watching it bow.',
  real:'Fans, fridges and family dinners are f-territory. Feathers, falling leaves and foxes in storybooks all arrive pre-labelled with the sound — just say their names slowly.',
  play:'Paper-fan game: fold a paper fan (or use a real one), then power it with f-breaths only. Each gust must carry an f-word across the room: fish, frog, four. Race the fan against a feather — the breath that moves things is the right one.',
  check:'Ask for the sound and watch the mouth: teeth on lip, moving air, silent throat. Then say v — if the buzz arrives and the shape stays, both twins are understood.',
  tips:'If fish becomes "vish", the voice snuck in — hand on the throat, no buzz on f. And keep the teeth soft: a gentle bite makes wind, a hard bite makes nothing.'},
 'g-goat':{
  title:'Gg — Goat',one:'The growly throat sound with the voice on, anchored to the silliest face in the set.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Gg Lesson: Teach the G Sound with Goat (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Gg and its growly /g/ sound, taught with the goat card — canvas tracing, a giggle game and a G-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Gg and its growly sound with the goat card: your child traces the letter, plays the giggle game where laughing IS the sound, and feels /g/ buzzing right in the throat.',
  what:'Gg is the letter that stands for the first sound in goat — the back of the tongue lifts deep in the mouth and lets go with the voice growling.',
  mean:'G teaches that sounds have homes: /g/ lives at the very back, the deepest spot in the mouth. Children feel it in the throat, and body-deep sounds stick for good.',
  know:'Your child needs the throat-growl feel, a clean "guh" (no stretched "guh-uh"), and a few g-words beyond goat — go, gum, giggle — to prove the sound is theirs.',
  why:'/g/ drives go, get, game, giggle — the action words of a four-year-old. It is also the twin of /k/ (same spot, g buzzes, k does not) and grows up into a second soft job later; the hard growl comes first.',
  dev:'Back-of-tongue control (the same spot as k, now with voice), throat awareness, and playful volume control — growls can be big or tiny.',
  vocab:'g, goat, go, gum, giggle, garden',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Gg. It says g — a growl from way down: guh. The goat sound!"\n2) Finger-trace the Gg on the canvas — the circle and the hook — while growling softly.\n3) Play the giggle game below; laughing is rehearsal for g.\n4) Growl three g-things: goat, gum, gate. Hand on the throat to feel the buzz.\n5) Finish with one enormous silly goat growl — noise allowed.',
  say:'"Put your hand on your throat. Now growl like a tiny goat: guh, guh. Feel it shake? That is g! G says guh, like goat."',
  sayhow:'Back of the tongue lifts high and deep, then drops with the voice on: "guh". One drop, one sound — if it drags into "guh-uh", snap it shorter.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Gg sits beside a smiling white goat with little horns, and the word "goat" is printed below — the set\u2019s comedians, wearing the growliest letter.',
  ask:'"Where do you feel g — lips, tongue or throat?" "Does it buzz?" "Growl me a g-word."',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "g-g-g-goat!" like a machine starting, or go straight for the joke: "giggle starts with g — g-g-giggle!" while collapsing into one.',
  real:'Gardens, gates and gum all grow g-words on demand. A farm visit or a goat video supplies the animal itself; giggling supplies the sound — free, unlimited, and already loved.',
  play:'Giggle game: the grown-up tries — really tries — NOT to laugh while saying g-words: goat, gum, gate. The child\u2019s job is to make it impossible: silly faces, goat noises, fake sneezes. Every giggle that escapes must be named: "that was a g-giggle!"',
  check:'Ask "what sound does G make?" and listen for the growl, not "jee". Then try k — same spot, no buzz. If the throat sorts them apart, both letters are learning-proof.',
  tips:'If g and k swap, the throat hand is the referee: g buzzes, k stays quiet. And skip soft g (giraffe) for now — the goat owns this card; giraffes can visit another year.'},
 'h-hat':{
  title:'Hh — Hat',one:'The pure breath sound with no shape and no voice, anchored to the word every head wears.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Hh Lesson: Teach the H Sound with Hat (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Hh and its breathy /h/ sound, taught with the hat card — canvas tracing, a mirror-fog game and an H-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Hh and its breath sound with the hat card: your child traces the letter, fogs a mirror (or a window) with h-breaths, and learns the quietest letter in the whole set.',
  what:'Hh is the letter that stands for the first sound in hat — a soft puff of air, no mouth shape, no voice: just breath with a letter on it.',
  mean:'H is the alphabet\u2019s whisper: it proves that some sounds are almost nothing at all. Learning to hear almost-nothing is real listening training — the hardest listening there is.',
  know:'Your child needs to feel the h-puff on their own hand, hear the difference between "hat" and "at" (one breath changes everything), and keep the puff gentle.',
  why:'/h/ starts hand, help, home, hungry — words a child says every single day. It is also the sound children most often drop ("at" for hat), and playful breath games bring it back without a single correction.',
  dev:'Breath control, gentle air awareness, and the fine listening that separates a puff from silence.',
  vocab:'h, hat, hand, house, hot, hello',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Hh. It is only breath — hhh, like warming your hands. The hat sound!"\n2) Finger-trace the Hh on the canvas: two tall posts and a bridge, while huffing softly.\n3) Play the mirror-fog game below — the breath becomes something you can see.\n4) Huff three h-things: hat, hand, house. Small puffs, clear words.\n5) Finish by whispering "hat" without the h, then with it — hear the magic puff arrive.',
  say:'"Breathe out warm air on your hands, like fogging a window: hhh. That is h! H says hhh, like hat. Warm your hands with the sound."',
  sayhow:'Open the mouth slightly and sigh warm air: "hhh". No voice, no shape, no effort — the laziest sound in English. If it becomes "kuh", the throat woke up; relax it again.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Hh sits beside a blue fedora hat with a red band, and the word "hat" is printed below — the letter that is barely a sound, on the card that is easy to wear.',
  ask:'"Can you feel the warm air?" "Is h loud or quiet?" "Say hat — now say at. What changed?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "hhhhat!" with a proper fog-cloud, or invent the game nobody taught: huffing at every window in the house and drawing h-letters in the fog.',
  real:'Bath steam, cold-morning breath and hot soup all make real h-clouds. Story wolves huff famously. And every hello, hat and hand is one puff away from practice.',
  play:'Mirror-fog game: hold a hand mirror (or use any window) and fog it with h-breaths. Before the fog fades, draw a big H in it with a fingertip and name an h-word — hat, hand, house. Fog, draw, name; fog, draw, name. The window does the scoring.',
  check:'Show the card tomorrow and ask for the sound: a soft, visible breath is the pass. Then say "at" and "hat" back to back — if your child hears the difference, the dropped-h habit is already curing itself.',
  tips:'Never chase the dropped h with corrections — huff the breath first, then add the word, and the h returns on its own. Whispering h on purpose also works: quiet sounds make children listen harder.'},
 'i-igloo':{
  title:'Ii — Igloo',one:'The tiniest vowel in the set — a quick, tight "ih" anchored to the snow house.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Ii Lesson: Teach the I Sound with Igloo (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Ii and its short /i/ sound, taught with the igloo card — canvas tracing, an itch game and an I-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Ii and its quick "ih" sound with the igloo card: your child traces the letter, plays the itch game that pulls the sound out naturally, and meets the smallest, quickest vowel in the alphabet.',
  what:'Ii is the letter that stands for the first sound in igloo — a short, tight "ih" made with the mouth barely open and the tongue high and forward.',
  mean:'I proves that vowels come in sizes: /a/ is a wide-open house, /i/ is a tiny snow hut. The mouth barely moves, the sound barely lasts — and it is still a whole letter.',
  know:'Your child needs to hear the quick "ih" as different from "eye" (the letter name) and from "ee", and to say it inside little words: igloo, in, insect, it.',
  why:'Short /i/ runs the small words that hold sentences together — it, in, is, big, him. A child who owns "ih" can soon read the glue-words that make sentences stick.',
  dev:'Tiny-movement vowel control (the opposite skill of wide-open a), quick sound production, and listening for small differences between close vowels.',
  vocab:'i, igloo, in, insect, it, inch',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Ii. It says ih — quick and small, like the start of igloo."\n2) Finger-trace the Ii on the canvas: one tall stick and a dot — the shortest letter-shape, the shortest sound.\n3) Play the itch game below; "ih" arrives with the scratching.\n4) Say three tiny i-words together: in, it, insect. Keep the sound quick.\n5) Finish with the inch test: move one small inch across the floor per i-word.',
  say:'"Make your mouth tiny — barely open: ih. That is I\u2019s sound, the igloo one. Say it quick like a tickle: ih, ih, igloo."',
  sayhow:'Mouth barely open, tongue high and forward, one quick beat: "ih". It is the shortest vowel — over and fast. If it stretches into "ee", tighten everything and try again.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Ii sits beside a snowy ice igloo with a round doorway, and the word "igloo" is printed below — a house built from the same stuff as the sound: small, quick, cool.',
  ask:'"Is your mouth big or tiny for ih?" "What is the picture on the card?" "Can you say three little i-words?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "ih-ih-igloo!" with tiny nibbling mouth moves, or report honestly: "insect starts with ih — I have one in my tummy." Both count.',
  real:'Insects in the garden, ink on fingertips, itchy jumpers — "ih" hides in daily life. Inside-and-on top of that: every "in", "it" and "is" your child hears all day is a secret i-drill.',
  play:'Itch game: pretend something is itchy — an arm, a nose, a knee. Scratch and announce: "ih — itchy!" Then scratch-itch other things and name them: an itchy insect, an itchy igloo, an itchy inchworm. Whoever scratches the silliest body part wins the round.',
  check:'Show the card the next day and ask for the sound: a quick "ih" (not "eye", not "ee") is the pass. Then hide the card and say "igloo" — if the first sound arrives tight and fast, the vowel is owned.',
  tips:'Short /i/ and long /i/ (ice, eye) are different jobs — keep this card\u2019s sound tiny and quick. If "eegloo" keeps coming, whisper it: quiet forces the mouth small.'},
 'j-jam':{
  title:'Jj — Jam',one:'The jumping tongue sound with the voice on, anchored to the stickiest word in the set.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Jj Lesson: Teach the J Sound with Jam (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Jj and its jumping /j/ sound, taught with the jam card — canvas tracing, a jelly-wobble game and a J-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Jj and its jumping sound with the jam card: your child traces the letter, wobbles like jelly through a j-word game, and feels the tongue jump that every j-word starts with.',
  what:'Jj is the letter that stands for the first sound in jam — the tongue starts behind the teeth and jumps forward with the voice buzzing: j-j-jam.',
  mean:'J is a movement sound: it does not sit still like m or s, it jumps. Children who learn j as motion — jump, jiggle, jog — are learning the sound and the action at once.',
  know:'Your child needs the quick tongue jump with voice on, a clean "juh", and a jar-full of j-words: jam, jump, juice, jog.',
  why:'/j/ opens jump, joy, jam and jacket — the energy words of childhood. It is also the twin of /ch/ (same spot, j buzzes, ch puffs), a distinction worth feeling early.',
  dev:'Front-tongue jump control, voice awareness, and syllable play — j-words tend to be bouncy, and bouncy words are rhythm practice in disguise.',
  vocab:'j, jam, jump, juice, jog, jelly',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Jj. The tongue jumps — j! It says the jam sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Jj on the canvas: the hook jumps down below the line, like the tongue jumping forward.\n3) Play the jelly-wobble game below — every j-word wobbles.\n4) Jump-say three j-things: jam, jump, juice. One small jump each.\n5) Finish with a whole-body jelly wobble and a loud "j-j-jam!"',
  say:'"Put your tongue up, then let it jump out: juh. That is j! J says juh, like jam. Jump the sound: j, j, jam."',
  sayhow:'Tongue tip starts behind the top teeth and springs forward with the voice buzzing: "juh". Quick and springy — one jump, no slide into "zh".',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Jj sits beside a strawberry jam jar with a red gingham lid, and the word "jam" is printed below — the stickiest card on the shelf, for the springiest sound.',
  ask:'"Does your tongue jump or sit still for j?" "Is your throat buzzing?" "What j-word makes you wobble best?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "j-j-jam!" with real jumps, or make the breakfast connection unprompted: "juice starts with j — pour the j-sound!"',
  real:'Jam, juice and jelly live at the breakfast table; jumps and jiggles live everywhere. Jackets, jeans and jigsaws fill the rest of the day — every one a j-word asking to be said slowly.',
  play:'Jelly-wobble game: whenever anyone says a j-word, everyone wobbles like a plate of jelly — jam! wobble. jump! wobble. jog! wobble. The grown-up\u2019s job is to say j-words fast; the child\u2019s job is to keep wobbling without falling over. Falling over is, of course, a j too: j-just get back up.',
  check:'Ask for the sound tomorrow: a springy "juh" with a buzzing throat is the pass. Then try ch — same spot in the mouth, puff instead of buzz. If the swap lands, the tongue has learned two letters for the price of one.',
  tips:'If j drifts to a y-sound, slow the jump down and exaggerate it. And keep it English-juh: in some languages j whispers differently — this card teaches the jumping English j.'},
 'k-kite':{
  title:'Kk — Kite',one:'The same back click as c, now wearing its own letter — anchored to the windiest toy.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Kk Lesson: Teach the K Sound with Kite (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Kk and its /k/ click, taught with the kite card — canvas tracing, a key-turn game and a K-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Kk and its click sound with the kite card: your child traces the letter, turns imaginary keys that unlock k-words, and learns the big spelling secret that two letters can share one sound.',
  what:'Kk is the letter that stands for the first sound in kite — the very same back-of-the-mouth click that c makes in cat, here in its own shape.',
  mean:'K is the alphabet’s spelling lesson in disguise: the click your child already owns now has a second written home. Meeting it as a fun fact — never a test — keeps both spellings friendly for life.',
  know:'Your child needs to recognise the click as the same one c makes, say it cleanly for k-words — kite, kick, key — and feel proud rather than confused about the two coats.',
  why:'/k/ keeps kick, key, kitchen and kind running. Meeting it as "c\u2019s twin" early prevents the classic later tear: "but why does cat start with c and kite with k?"',
  dev:'Back-click control (second visit), sound identity beyond letter shapes, and the flexible thinking that lets one sound belong to two letters.',
  vocab:'k, kite, kick, key, king, kiss',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Kk. It says k — the same click as cat, in a new coat: kite!"\n2) Finger-trace the Kk on the canvas: one tall post, one arm and one leg — a letter built like a little person.\n3) Play the key-turn game below; every turn unlocks a k-word.\n4) Click three k-things: kite, king, keys. Listen for the same click as cat.\n5) Finish by saying cat, then kite — and announcing: "same sound, two letters!"',
  say:'"Here is a secret: K and C make the very same click. K just wears its own coat. Say it: k, k, kite — the wind sound!"',
  sayhow:'Identical to hard c: back of the tongue lifts and releases, voice off: "k". One dry click, no vowel trailing.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Kk sits beside a colourful diamond kite with two bows on its tail, and the word "kite" is printed below — the flying toy that owns the second click-spelling.',
  ask:'"What sound does K make?" "Is it the same sound as cat?" "What k-word should we unlock next?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "k-k-k-kite!" with three tugs, or deliver the lesson better than any adult: "c and k are twins!" — a fact they will repeat to everyone.',
  real:'Keys at every door, kicks in every garden, kings in every storybook. Windy days are kite days; windy days are also k-practice days.',
  play:'Key-turn game: mime holding a giant ring of keys. Each turn — click! — must unlock a k-word before the imaginary door opens: kite, king, kiss, kitchen. Open the door, peek inside, close it, pick the next key. Ten keys, ten words, one very heavy ring.',
  check:'Ask "what sound does K make?" — the goal is the click AND the twin-fact: "same as c!" Then hide the card and ask for kite, kick, key in a row; three clean clicks mean the second coat fits.',
  tips:'Do not make the c/k difference a problem — it is a fun fact, not a quiz. Say "twins with two coats" once, play the keys, and let the spelling sort itself out years later.'},
 'l-lion':{
  title:'Ll — Lion',one:'The singing tongue-tip lift — the one consonant that can hold a tune.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Ll Lesson: Teach the L Sound with Lion (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Ll and its singing /l/ sound, taught with the lion card — canvas tracing, a ladder-climb game and an L-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Ll and its singing sound with the lion card: your child traces the letter, climbs an imaginary ladder one l-word per rung, and keeps the tongue tip up — the whole secret of l.',
  what:'Ll is the letter that stands for the first sound in lion — the tongue tip lifts behind the top teeth and the voice sings around its sides: l-l-lion.',
  mean:'L shows that consonants can be vowels’ warm-up acts: the voice flows AROUND the sides of the lifted tongue instead of stopping. That side-flow is why l blends so smoothly into the vowels that follow it.',
  know:'Your child needs the tongue-tip-up feel, the singing hold, and a pride of l-words — lion, leg, love, lullaby — spoken with the tip visibly lifting.',
  why:'/l/ starts look, love, like and lunch — the warmest words in a small child\u2019s day. It is also one of the last sounds to mature, so gentle games now beat corrections later.',
  dev:'Tongue-tip lift and hold, sustained voicing (singing a consonant), and mirror self-checking — with l, the tongue tip is visible when done right.',
  vocab:'l, lion, leg, lip, love, lullaby',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Ll. The tongue tip goes up and sings — l! The lion sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Ll on the canvas: one tall post and one foot — the same shape as a tongue tip lifting, say it while you draw it.\n3) Play the ladder-climb game below — one rung, one l-word.\n4) Sing three l-things: lion, leg, love. Hold each l as long as breath allows.\n5) Finish with the mirror check: tongue tip visible at the top teeth means a true l.',
  say:'"Put your tongue tip up behind your teeth and let it sing: llll. That is l! L says lll, like lion. Climb the sound: l, l, lion."',
  sayhow:'Tongue tip pressed lightly behind the top teeth, voice on, air flowing around the sides: "lll". It holds — sing it long. If it turns into "w" ("yion"), the tip is not lifting.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Ll sits beside a golden lion with a great dark mane, and the word "lion" is printed below — and the mane curls exactly like a lowercase l, a shape-lesson hiding in the picture.',
  ask:'"Can you see your tongue tip?" "How long can your l sing?" "What l-word should we climb to next?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "lllllion!" with a five-second hold, or point at the mane and shout the shape-fact: "the lion has little l\u2019s all over his head!"',
  real:'Ladders, lifts and legs; lips, lamps and love. The zoo lion roars once and the lullaby sings it again at bedtime — the sound is everywhere a child is.',
  play:'Ladder-climb game: stand tall and climb an invisible ladder, one rung per l-word — leg, lip, log, lift. Each climb ends on a shelf where an l-thing waits to be named. Climb high enough and the top shelf holds the grand prize: a lion (soft toy recommended, roaring optional).',
  check:'Ask for the sound and watch the mirror: a visible tongue tip with a singing hold is the pass. If l arrives as w, slow down, exaggerate the tip lift, and let the mirror do the correcting — no words needed.',
  tips:'/l/ is a late bloomer — "yion" is a stage, not a fault. Keep the games silly, keep the tip visible, and never practice longer than the giggles last.'},
 'm-moon':{
  title:'Mm — Moon',one:'The humming lip sound every child made before any word — anchored to the bedtime sky.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Mm Lesson: Teach the M Sound with Moon (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Mm and its humming /m/ sound, taught with the moon card — canvas tracing, a tasty-hum game and an M-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Mm and its humming sound with the moon card: your child traces the letter, hums the tasty hum before every pretend snack, and meets the sound their mouth has known since babyhood.',
  what:'Mm is the letter that stands for the first sound in moon — lips pressed together, voice humming straight through them: mmm.',
  mean:'M is the sound children make before words: "mmm" for tasty, for thinking, for calling mum. Learning m is not learning something new — it is naming something already owned.',
  know:'Your child needs the closed-lip hum, the feel of the buzz on the lips, and a moonful of m-words — moon, mum, milk, more — hummed into full words.',
  why:'/m/ opens mum, milk, mine and more — the first-word family of almost every child. It is also the twin of /n/ (lips versus tongue tip), a difference the pinch-test makes loud and funny.',
  dev:'Closed-lip humming, voice awareness through the nose-versus-lips contrast, and connecting an owned sound to its written letter.',
  vocab:'m, moon, mum, milk, more, mmm',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Mm. Close your lips and hum — mmm. The moon sound!"\n2) Finger-trace the Mm on the canvas: four little mountain legs, humming the whole way across.\n3) Play the tasty-hum game below — the hum becomes a meal.\n4) Hum-say three m-things: moon, milk, muffin. Lips closed until the vowel.\n5) Finish with the longest hum of the day, landed softly on "moon".',
  say:'"Close your lips and sing through them: mmmm. That is m! M says mmm, like moon — and like the sound you make when dinner is delicious."',
  sayhow:'Lips fully closed, voice humming through the nose and lips: "mmm". Hold it long, then open into the word — mmm-moon. If the lips part early, the m disappears; keep them sealed.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Mm sits beside a smiling full moon with soft craters, and the word "moon" is printed below — the night-sky friend for the sound children hum at bedtime anyway.',
  ask:'"Are your lips open or closed for m?" "Can you hum with your mouth shut?" "What m-word would you eat right now?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "mmmmoon!" with closed lips then a big open finish, or rate your cooking honestly: "mmm! that means m-words are coming."',
  real:'Mealtimes are m-festivals: mmm before every bite of mash, melon, muffin. Bedtime adds the moon itself, plus lullabies hummed on m — the sound already lives in your child\u2019s favourite moments.',
  play:'Tasty-hum game: lay out pretend snacks (real ones work too). Before tasting each one, the eater must hum approval — "mmm!" — then name it with the hum attached: mmm-melon, mmm-muffin. Swap chef and critic roles. The tastiest-sounding m-word wins the last bite.',
  check:'Show the card tomorrow and ask for the sound: a closed-lip hum is an instant pass — it is the hardest sound to get wrong. Then try n, and pinch the nose mid-hum: the buzz stops. M and n, sorted by nose.',
  tips:'If m-words start losing their front sound ("oon" for moon), hum the m and keep the lips closed until the last possible moment. The lips re-learn in one game.'},
 'n-nest':{
  title:'Nn — Nest',one:'The nose-hum twin of m — tongue tip up, voice buzzing — anchored to the cosiest picture in the set.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Nn Lesson: Teach the N Sound with Nest (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Nn and its nose-hum /n/ sound, taught with the nest card — canvas tracing, a night-stars game and an N-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Nn and its nose-hum sound with the nest card: your child traces the letter, switches on pretend stars one n-word at a time, and learns how the nose joins the alphabet.',
  what:'Nn is the letter that stands for the first sound in nest — a quick tap of the tongue that turns into a hum, the sound a bottle makes as it empties: nnn.',
  mean:'N shares one doorway with m: the nose. The only thing that changes is the tongue — resting at the lips for m, lifted behind the teeth for n. One body, two sounds, one fingertip apart.',
  know:'Your child needs the tongue-tip-up hum, the nose-buzz feel (pinch test!), and a nest-full of n-words: nest, nose, night, nine.',
  why:'/n/ carries no, night, nine and napkin — words with real power in a child\u2019s day. It is also m\u2019s twin, and sorting the two by tongue spot now prevents years of m/n spelling swaps.',
  dev:'Tongue-tip precision while humming, nose-versus-lips discrimination, and the fine mouth-awareness that separates close twin sounds.',
  vocab:'n, nest, nose, night, nine, nap',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Nn. Tongue tip up, hum through your nose — nnn. The nest sound!"\n2) Finger-trace the Nn on the canvas: two posts and a bridge, one nnn-hum per stroke.\n3) Play the night-stars game below — every n-word lights a star.\n4) Hum three n-things: nest, nose, night. Tongue tip stays high.\n5) Finish with the pinch test: hum n, pinch the nose, feel the buzz stop. Magic.',
  say:'"Tap your tongue behind your teeth and hum — nnn. That is n! N says nnn, like nest. Feel it buzzing in your nose? N, n, nest."',
  sayhow:'Tongue tip pressed behind the top teeth, voice humming through the nose, mouth lightly closed or open: "nnn". The lips stay apart — if they close, you made an m.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Nn sits beside a brown nest holding three pale eggs, and the word "nest" is printed below — three eggs, free counting practice, riding on the sound.',
  ask:'"Where is your tongue for n?" "What happens if I pinch your nose while you hum?" "Name an n-word in this room."',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "nnnest!" with the nose-scrunch of a proper hum, or demand the pinch test on repeat — the buzz-stop is preschool comedy gold.',
  real:'Night-time is n-time: night, no, nose-wipe, nine bedtime hugs. Birds\u2019 nests on walks, napkins at dinner, and the word no (said by both parties) keep the sound in rotation.',
  play:'Night-stars game: the room goes to night-time (lights can stay on; pretend dark is better). Each n-word said switches on one imaginary star: night… nose… nine… nest. Fill the whole ceiling, then count the stars — nine is, naturally, the best answer.',
  check:'Ask for the sound and watch the tongue: tip up, nose buzzing. Then the referee game: m and n back to back — lips for m, tongue for n. If your child can show both, the twins are sorted.',
  tips:'Swap hunts work better than corrections here: say nest, nose and name in a row and let your child point to their nose or their lips each time they hear where the hum sits. The pointing finger learns faster than the correcting mouth — and no inside the nest stays the best n-word of all.'},
 'o-octopus':{
  title:'Oo — Octopus',one:'The round short "oh" — the letter that draws its own mouth shape — anchored to eight friendly arms.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Oo Lesson: Teach the O Sound with Octopus (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Oo and its round short /o/ sound, taught with the octopus card — canvas tracing, a surprise-box game and an O-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Oo and its round sound with the octopus card: your child traces the letter, plays the surprise-box game where every opening is an "O!", and learns the one letter that is also a perfect mouth shape.',
  what:'Oo is the letter that stands for the first sound in octopus — a short, round "oh" made with the lips curled into a little circle.',
  mean:'O is the alphabet\u2019s self-portrait: the letter looks like the mouth shape it needs. Show the letter, make the mouth — the shape teaches itself, which is why o is a favourite first vowel.',
  know:'Your child needs the round-lip "oh", kept short (this is octopus /o/, not open /oh/), and a basket of o-words: octopus, on, off, ox.',
  why:'/o/ powers on, off, hot, not, stop — the words that run a four-year-old\u2019s day, including the most powerful one: stop. Short and round, and sentences start working.',
  dev:'Lip rounding control, letter-shape to mouth-shape mapping, and keeping a vowel short when the letter name wants to stretch it.',
  vocab:'o, octopus, on, off, ox, hot',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Oo. Round your mouth like this letter: oh! The octopus sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Oo on the canvas — a perfect circle, drawn while the lips hold their own perfect circle.\n3) Play the surprise-box game below; every lid-lift is a natural O.\n4) Round-say three o-things: on, off, octopus. Keep the sound short and round.\n5) Finish with eight arm-wiggles — one per arm, one per o-word.',
  say:'"Make your lips into a little circle — oh! That is O\u2019s sound, the octopus one. Say it round: oh, oh, octopus."',
  sayhow:'Lips rounded into a small circle, voice on, sound kept short: "oh". No glide up into "oh-uh" — clip it clean. The letter draws the mouth; the mouth draws the sound.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Oo sits beside a smiling purple octopus with curling arms, and the word "octopus" is printed below — eight arms for eight practice rounds, all starting with the same round sound.',
  ask:'"Is your mouth round or flat for o?" "What does the letter look like?" "Say on — now off. Both start with...?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "o-o-o-octopus!" with lips like a goldfish, or connect the shape forever: "O looks like my mouth when I say it!"',
  real:'On-and-off switches are o-controllers disguised as furniture. Hot and not, stop and go, octopuses in the bath — the round sound runs the day\u2019s biggest moments.',
  play:'Surprise-box game: fill an imaginary box (or a real one) with mystery objects. Open the lid — "O!" — then name what is inside, starting with the sound you just made: an octopus, an orange, an ox. Close, shake, reopen. The louder the O, the bigger the surprise.',
  check:'Show the card tomorrow and ask for the sound: a short round "oh" is the pass; a stretched "oh-ohh" means the long O visited — clip it short and move on. The surprise-box reopen is the quickest repair.',
  tips:'Short /o/ (octopus, on) and long /o/ (open, over) are different jobs. Keep this one round and clipped — the long O has its own cards elsewhere on the path.'},
 'p-pig':{
  title:'Pp — Pig',one:'The little lip-puff with no voice — b\u2019s quiet twin — anchored to the snuffliest farm animal.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Pp Lesson: Teach the P Sound with Pig (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Pp and its puffing /p/ sound, taught with the pig card — canvas tracing, a popcorn game and a P-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Pp and its puff sound with the pig card: your child traces the letter, pops like corn on every p-word, and catches the invisible puff of air that separates p from its loud twin b.',
  what:'Pp is the letter that stands for the first sound in pig — lips pressed together, then a little burst of air with no voice: p-p-pig.',
  mean:'P is invisible — nothing wiggles in the mouth to show it happened. So this lesson teaches the tissue trick and the mirror check: children learn to catch sounds they cannot see.',
  know:'Your child needs the soft lip burst, the silent throat (p does not buzz), and a pen of p-words: pig, pop, park, pink.',
  why:'/p/ runs please, play, park and popcorn — the polite-and-playful heart of a child\u2019s day. It is also b\u2019s twin, and the puff test learned here keeps the two spellings apart forever.',
  dev:'Lip-burst control, voice-off awareness (the b/p contrast), and instrument-based self-checking — tissue and mirror as the child\u2019s own measuring tools.',
  vocab:'p, pig, pop, park, pink, popcorn',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Pp. Lips together, little puff — p! The pig sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Pp on the canvas: one tall post and one round belly, puffing with each curve.\n3) Play the popcorn game below — every pop is a p-word.\n4) Puff-say three p-things: pig, pink, park. Throat silent, lips busy.\n5) Finish with the tissue test: p moves the tissue, b does not. Science!',
  say:'"Press your lips together and let a tiny puff out: p! That is P\u2019s sound, the pig one. Pop the sound: p, p, pig."',
  sayhow:'Lips sealed, then opened with a small air burst and no voice: "puh". Keep the puff small and quick — a big dramatic blast is fine for learning, quiet for talking.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Pp sits beside a smiling pink pig, and the word "pig" is printed below — the snout-nosed teacher for the sound with no voice and plenty of puff.',
  ask:'"Does your throat buzz for p?" "What happens to a tissue when you say p?" "What p-word would you plant in the garden?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "p-p-p-pig!" with popcorn jumps, or run the tissue experiment on the whole family — p, b, f, everything gets tested.',
  real:'Parks, picnics and puddles; pigs at the farm, penguins at the zoo. Popcorn nights put the sound right in the bowl — pop is the best p-word ever invented.',
  play:'Popcorn game: everyone crouches low like a seed in the pan. The grown-up pretends to pour oil, shake the pan and turn up the heat — and when the heat is HOT, each child pops up with a p-word: pop! pink! pig! park! Pop, say the word, sink back down, wait for the next shake. The biggest pop wins the pan.',
  check:'Ask for the sound and hold the tissue: a visible jump with a silent throat is the pass. Then say b — same lips, buzzing, tissue still. If your child can feel both twins, p is fully learned.',
  tips:'If pig comes out as big, the voice switched on — hand on the throat proves p stays quiet. Exaggerate the puff while learning; the small everyday p arrives on its own.'},
 'q-queen':{
  title:'Qq — Queen',one:'Two sounds glued together — /kw/ — anchored to the crowned face every preschooler bows to.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Qq Lesson: Teach the Q Sound with Queen (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Qq and its two-sound /kw/, taught with the queen card — canvas tracing, a quick-flash game and a Q-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Qq, the only letter that carries two sounds at once: a /k/ click straight into a /w/ puff. Your child traces the letter, plays the quick-flash game, and learns why q never walks alone.',
  what:'Qq stands for the first sound in queen — /kw/, two sounds glued into one burst: the k click of cat followed instantly by the w of wind.',
  mean:'Q is the set\u2019s first two-sound letter — proof that letters can be teams. And q has a famous friend: almost every q in English brings the letter u, because u helps make the w.',
  know:'Your child needs to feel the click-then-puff as ONE motion, hear /kw/ at the start of queen, quick and quiet, and know that q and u are a package deal.',
  why:'/kw/ is rare but mighty: queen, quick, quilt and quiet are story-time royalty. Learning the two-in-one burst now makes the strangest letter feel like a trick, not a problem.',
  dev:'Sound blending at its smallest (two sounds, one burst), back-click plus lip-round coordination, and letter-team awareness (q + u forever).',
  vocab:'q, queen, quick, quiet, quilt, quack',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Qq. It says kw — a click and a puff in one: queen!"\n2) Finger-trace the Qq on the canvas: the circle, then the tail — and draw the u next to it, because q never travels alone.\n3) Play the quick-flash game below; every flash ends with a /kw/ word.\n4) Burst-say three q-things: queen, quick, quack. One click-puff each.\n5) Finish with a royal bow and a very quiet "quiet" — the hardest q-word, said perfectly.',
  say:'"Make the k click and blow it into a puff of wind — kwuh! That is Q\u2019s sound: two sounds in one. Say it royal: kw, kw, queen."',
  sayhow:'Back click (/k/) released straight into rounded lips (/w/), voice on: "kwuh". Said fast it is one burst; said slow it falls apart. Speed is the glue.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Qq sits beside a smiling queen with a golden crown and a purple gown, and the word "queen" is printed below — the majesty that owns the two-sound letter.',
  ask:'"How many sounds does q make?" "What letter always comes after q?" "Can you say quick as fast as possible?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "kw-kw-kween!" with royal waving, or spot the pattern forever after: "that word has q — where is its friend u?"',
  real:'Queens and quacks rule story-time; quilts wait at grandparents\u2019 houses. Quick and quiet run every game of hide-and-seek — two q-words hiding in plain sight.',
  play:'Quick-flash game: the grown-up calls an action, the child does it QUICKLY — and announces each one with the /kw/ burst: clap kw-ickly! touch toes kw-ickly! sit down kw-ickly! Finish the round with the hardest command of all: "now be kw-iet" — a royal whisper wins the crown.',
  check:'Ask what sound q makes and listen for the glue: "kw" as one burst (not "k…wuh" in two pieces) is the pass. Then ask what comes after q in a word — "u!" means the team-fact landed too.',
  tips:'Say /kw/ as one burst, not two separate sounds — slow it down only to demonstrate, then speed it back up. And enjoy the oddness: q is the weird one, and children love being insiders on the weird one.'},
 'r-rabbit':{
  title:'Rr — Rabbit',one:'The curling sound that touches nothing — anchored to the animal children say best and adults explain worst.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Rr Lesson: Teach the R Sound with Rabbit (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Rr and its curling /r/ sound, taught with the rabbit card — canvas tracing, a race-engine game and an R-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Rr and its curling sound with the rabbit card: your child traces the letter, revs like a race car, and learns the sound made where no mirror can see — pure listening, pure tongue.',
  what:'Rr is the letter that stands for the first sound in rabbit — the tongue curls up toward the roof of the mouth and touches NOTHING, while the voice runs: r-r-rabbit.',
  mean:'R is the alphabet\u2019s invisible one: no lips to watch, no tongue tip to see. Children who learn r by feel (the growl, the engine) beat children who learn it by instruction.',
  know:'Your child needs the curled-tongue feel without touching, the running voice, and a warren of r-words: rabbit, run, red, rain.',
  why:'/r/ drives run, red, ride and ready — the movement words of childhood. It is also famously the LAST sound to mature, so early playful exposure plants it without pressure.',
  dev:'Tongue-body control without visual feedback, sustained voicing, and trusting listening over looking — the deepest mouth-awareness lesson in the set.',
  vocab:'r, rabbit, run, red, rain, road',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Rr. Curl your tongue up — but touch nothing! It says the rabbit sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Rr on the canvas: the tall post with its little arm — the arm curls, the tongue curls.\n3) Play the race-engine game below; the engine rev is the sound\u2019s natural home.\n4) Run-say three r-things: rabbit, rain, red. Tongue curled, voice driving.\n5) Finish with three bunny hops, one r-word per hop.',
  say:'"Curl your tongue up like a wave and let the engine run: rrrr. That is r! R says rrr, like rabbit. Rev the sound: r, r, rabbit."',
  sayhow:'Tongue curled toward the roof of the mouth, close but touching nothing, voice on: "rrr". It can run like an engine. If it becomes w ("wabbit"), the tongue flattened — curl it again.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Rr sits beside a grey rabbit with long ears, and the word "rabbit" is printed below — the hoppiest animal in the set, carrying the sound children master last.',
  ask:'"Can your tongue touch anything for r?" "What does a race car say?" "What r-word hops?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "r-r-r-rabbit!" with full engine sounds, or request the growl game daily — revving is practice disguised as fun.',
  real:'Car rides are r-practice: roads, roundabouts, red lights, rain. Rabbit visits in storybooks, and every run around the garden is a chance to say run while doing it.',
  play:'Race-engine game: be the driver and the car. Start the engine — "rrr, rrr, rrrr" — then drive to a destination called out by the grown-up: a red cushion, a round table, the rabbit\u2019s burrow. Park, turn the engine off, pick a new r-destination. Traffic noises encouraged; r-words required at every red light.',
  check:'Ask for the sound: a running "rrr" with the tongue clearly NOT touching is the pass. If "wabbit" appears, stay calm — r is the last sound to settle, and growls and engines do the teaching without any corrections.',
  tips:'Skip the corrections entirely — \u201cwabbit\u201d is a normal stop on the way to r. Keep the engine games silly and frequent, and let the tongue arrive on its own schedule; months of gentle practice are perfectly normal.'},
 's-sun':{
  title:'Ss — Sun',one:'The set\u2019s marathon sound — a hiss with no voice that lasts a whole breath — anchored to the morning sky.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Ss Lesson: Teach the S Sound with Sun (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Ss and its hissing /s/ sound, taught with the sun card — canvas tracing, a sleepy-snake game and an S-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Ss and its hiss with the sun card: your child traces the letter, rocks a toy snake to sleep with the world\u2019s longest hiss, and lands the sound on a whole family of s-words.',
  what:'Ss is the letter that stands for the first sound in sun — teeth nearly closed, air hissing through, no voice: a sound that stretches as long as the breath does.',
  mean:'S is the sound children already own: every "ssss" for quiet, every snake impression, every sparkler. This lesson just attaches the letter to a sound the mouth has practiced for years.',
  know:'Your child needs the teeth-nearly-closed hiss, the long hold, and a sunshine of s-words: sun, sock, six, snake.',
  why:'/s/ starts see, so, stop, sorry — words that steer a whole day. It is also the whisper twin of /z/, and the throat-check learned here keeps sun and zoo in their own letters.',
  dev:'Sustained air control (the longest sound in the set), teeth-to-tongue-channel shaping, and voice-off awareness with a familiar favourite.',
  vocab:'s, sun, sock, six, snake, soap',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Ss. Teeth almost together, air hissing out — ssss! The sun sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Ss on the canvas — the curvy snake letter, drawn while hissing gently.\n3) Play the sleepy-snake game below; the hiss becomes a lullaby.\n4) Hiss three s-things: sun, sock, soap. Long hiss, short word.\n5) Finish with the hiss contest: the longest hiss picks the next s-word.',
  say:'"Make your teeth almost touch and hiss like a snake: ssss. That is s! S says ssss, like sun. Hiss it long: ssss… sun."',
  sayhow:'Teeth nearly closed, tongue close behind them, air pushed through the gap, voice off: "ssss". It holds as long as breath allows — the set\u2019s marathon sound.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Ss sits beside a smiling yellow sun with rounded rays, and the word "sun" is printed below — the morning friend that starts every sunny day with its sound.',
  ask:'"Is your throat quiet for s?" "How long can you hiss?" "What hisses in real life — a snake, a tyre, a sparkler?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "ssssun!" with a five-second hold, or organise the household hiss contest themselves — s brings out the competitor.',
  real:'Socks, soap, sandwiches and stairs; sun at wake-up, stars at bed-time. Snakes (real or toy) hiss on demand, and every "ssshh" for quiet is an s already in use.',
  play:'Sleepy-snake game: wrap a toy snake (or a scarf) in a blanket and rock it to sleep with the softest, longest hiss — "ssssss". But the moment the snake wakes, it must say an s-word before settling again: ssss-sock, ssss-sun, ssss-six. Rock, hiss, wake, word — until the snake finally sleeps for a full five-second hiss.',
  check:'Ask for the sound and listen for the hold: a clean long hiss, throat silent. Then try z — same hiss, voice buzzing. If your child can switch the buzz on and off, both twins are handled.',
  tips:'If sun becomes "zun", the throat woke early — hand on the throat, restart the hiss. And use the hold as a game timer: hiss-length contests do the repetition for you.'},
 't-turtle':{
  title:'Tt — Turtle',one:'The whispery tongue tap — d\u2019s quiet twin — anchored to the slowest animal on the path.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Tt Lesson: Teach the T Sound with Turtle (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Tt and its tapping /t/ sound, taught with the turtle card — canvas tracing, a train-ticket game and a T-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Tt and its quick tap with the turtle card: your child traces the letter, punches imaginary train tickets one t-word at a time, and feels the whisper-tap that never buzzes.',
  what:'Tt is the letter that stands for the first sound in turtle — a quick tongue tap just behind the top teeth with no voice: light, dry, over in a blink.',
  mean:'T is d\u2019s whisper twin: the same tap, the same spot, minus the buzz. Learning the pair by feel — tap with voice, tap without — is mouth-machinery knowledge for life.',
  know:'Your child needs the quick dry tap, the silent throat, and a turtle-pace list of t-words: turtle, toe, tree, ten.',
  why:'/t/ carries toe, tea, train and tick-tock — sounds a child hears from morning to night. It is also the most common ending tap in English (cat, sit, hot), so the tap learned here works at BOTH ends of words.',
  dev:'Tongue-tip speed, voice-off control (the d/t switch), and word-ending awareness — noticing that sounds can finish words too.',
  vocab:'t, turtle, toe, tree, ten, tick-tock',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Tt. Tap behind your teeth — quick and quiet: t! The turtle sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Tt on the canvas: one tall post and one roof, one tap per stroke.\n3) Play the train-ticket game below; every punch is a tap.\n4) Tap three t-things: turtle, toe, tea. Quick taps, slow words.\n5) Finish with the tick-tock finale: alternate t-t-t with d-d-d and feel the voice switch.',
  say:'"Tap your tongue just behind your teeth — t! Quick and quiet, like a clock. T says t, like turtle. Tick-tock the sound: t, t, turtle."',
  sayhow:'Tongue tip taps the ridge behind the top teeth and releases, voice off: "t". One dry tap — no buzz, no puff-drag. The throat stays silent the whole time.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Tt sits beside a smiling green turtle, and the word "turtle" is printed below — the slowest animal in the set, perfect for the tap that is over fastest.',
  ask:'"Does your throat buzz for t?" "Where does your tongue tap?" "What t-word ticks?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "t-t-t-turtle!" like a careful little clock, or report the great discovery: "t and d are the same tap — one just whispers!"',
  real:'Tick-tock clocks, taps at the door, toast and tea; turtles at the pond, toes in the bath. The tap hides at the END of words too: cat, hot, sit — say them slowly and catch it.',
  play:'Train-ticket game: build a train from chairs. The conductor punches each passenger\u2019s ticket — "t-t!" — and every ticket names a t-stop: Turtle Town, Tree Station, Ten Street. Choo-choo between stops, tap the tickets, announce the destinations. Late passengers must pay in t-words.',
  check:'Ask for the sound and watch the throat: a dry tap with no buzz is the pass. Then alternate with d — same tap, buzzing. If your child can feel the switch, the twins are clocked and sorted.',
  tips:'If turtle comes out "durdle", the voice crept in — hand on the throat restarts the whisper. And try t at the END of words too: cat, hot, sit — the tap works both shifts.'},
 'u-umbrella':{
  title:'Uu — Umbrella',one:'The lazy resting vowel — the relaxed "uh" — anchored to the rain-day friend.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Uu Lesson: Teach the U Sound with Umbrella (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Uu and its relaxed /u/ sound, taught with the umbrella card — canvas tracing, a lazy-rest game and a U-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Uu and its relaxed "uh" with the umbrella card: your child traces the letter, plays the lazy game where doing nothing IS the sound, and meets the easiest vowel the mouth can make.',
  what:'Uu is the letter that stands for the first sound in umbrella — the short, loose \u201cuh\u201d that asks nothing of the mouth: no stretch, no rounding, no shape.',
  mean:'U is the resting shape: the mouth\u2019s default. After wide-open a, half-open e and tiny i, the mouth simply lets go — and that letting-go is the sound.',
  know:'Your child needs the do-nothing mouth, a short "uh" (this is umbrella /u/, not unicorn /you/), and a puddle of u-words: up, under, us, umbrella.',
  why:'/u/ runs up, under, us and uh-oh — the words of climbing, hiding and small disasters. It is also the last short vowel on the path; owning it completes the vowel family.',
  dev:'Muscle relaxation as a skill (the opposite of every other vowel), short-vowel discipline, and noticing the mouth\u2019s resting position as a sound all by itself.',
  vocab:'u, umbrella, up, under, us, uh-oh',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Uu. Let your mouth go loose — uh! The umbrella sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Uu on the canvas: one cup shape, drawn while the mouth hangs loose.\n3) Play the lazy-rest game below; the sound arrives when nothing tries.\n4) Say three u-things: up, under, umbrella. Mouth lazy, sound short.\n5) Finish with an uh-oh moment: drop a pretend cup — "uh-OH!" — and laugh.',
  say:'"Let everything go loose — no stretching, no rounding: uh. That is U\u2019s sound, the umbrella one. Say it lazy: uh, uh, umbrella."',
  sayhow:'Mouth relaxed and barely open, tongue at rest, voice on: "uh". Nothing should feel stretched — if the lips round or spread, the vowel changed. Lazy is correct.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Uu sits beside a rainbow-striped umbrella, and the word "umbrella" is printed below — the brightest thing about a grey-rain sound.',
  ask:'"Is your mouth working hard or resting for uh?" "What do you say when you drop something?" "What goes up — and what goes under?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "uh-uh-umbrella!" with a proper shrug, or catch the sound mid-drama: "uh-oh! that starts with U!" — disaster and lesson in one.',
  real:'Up and under run the playground; uh-oh runs the kitchen. Umbrellas open on rain days, and every "uh-huh" and "uh-uh" in conversation is free practice.',
  play:'Lazy-rest game: slump into a big chair like the world\u2019s tiredest lion. From the slump, say u-words with the least possible effort: up… under… us… umbrella. The rule: whoever tries the HARDEST loses; the laziest, loosest "uh" wins. Finish with a giant pretend yawn — the laziest u of all.',
  check:'Show the card tomorrow and ask for the sound: a loose "uh" is the pass; a round "oo" or a stretchy "you" means long-u dropped by — restart lazy. Then hide the card and say umbrella slowly: the first sound should arrive without effort.',
  tips:'Short /u/ ("uh") and long /u/ ("you", as in unicorn) are different jobs — this card teaches the relaxed one. If umbrella drifts to "oo-mbrella", model three flat, lazy starts and let the shrug do the rest.'},
 'v-van':{
  title:'Vv — Van',one:'The loud half of a famous pair — the f shape with the voice switched on — anchored to the van beeping down the street.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Vv Lesson: Teach the V Sound with Van (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Vv and its buzzing /v/ sound, taught with the van card — canvas tracing, an invisible-violin game and a V-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Vv and its buzz with the van card: your child traces the letter, plays an invisible violin through a v-word concert, and switches the voice ON — the difference between fan and van.',
  what:'Vv is the letter that stands for the first sound in van — top teeth on the bottom lip, air buzzing through WITH the voice on: vvv.',
  mean:'V completes a famous pair: f whispers, v buzzes — same teeth, same lip, one switch. V is the first sound in the set taught entirely as a switch: find the f, turn the voice on.',
  know:'Your child needs the teeth-on-lip shape WITH the throat buzzing, the f-to-v switch as a game, and a streetful of v-words: van, vet, violin, vase.',
  why:'/v/ drives van, video, visit and five — wait, five hides v in the MIDDLE. That is v\u2019s second secret: it loves word endings and middles (five, love, over) as much as beginnings.',
  dev:'Teeth-lip coordination with voicing, the on/off voice switch (f/v), and word-position awareness — v works at the front, middle and end.',
  vocab:'v, van, vet, violin, vase, five',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Vv. Teeth on the lip — but this time it BUZZES: vvv! The van sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Vv on the canvas: two slopes meeting in a point, one buzz down each slope.\n3) Play the violin game below — every bow stroke hums.\n4) Buzz three v-things: van, vase, violin. Feel the throat running.\n5) Finish with the f-v switch: fff…vvv…fff…vvv. The throat is the switch.',
  say:'"Bite your lip softly like f — but turn your voice on: vvv. Feel it buzz? That is v! V says vvv, like van."',
  sayhow:'Top teeth resting on the bottom lip, air through, voice ON: "vvv". It holds and hums. Same shape as f; the only difference is the buzzing throat.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Vv sits beside a white delivery van with a friendly face, and the word "van" is printed below — the vehicle that drives the buzz past your window most mornings.',
  ask:'"Is your throat buzzing or quiet for v?" "What is the difference between fan and van?" "What v-word drives?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "vvvvan!" with a full-buzz engine, or deliver the twin-fact unprompted: "f whispers, v buzzes — I can feel it in my neck!"',
  real:'Vans, vets and visits; violins at concerts, vases on shelves. Five and love carry v inside, and every video call starts with someone saying vee very clearly.',
  play:'Violin game: prop an invisible violin under the chin, take an invisible bow, and play. Each bow stroke must hum a v-word: vvv-van, vvv-violin, vvv-five. Request songs by v-name, take turns as conductor and soloist, and end the concert with the longest vvv in the family\u2019s history. Standing ovation optional; buzzing mandatory.',
  check:'Ask for the sound and hold the throat: buzzing teeth-on-lip is the pass. Then run the switch test — fff (quiet) to vvv (buzz) and back. If your child can flip the switch, fan and van will never blur again.',
  tips:'If van arrives as fan, the voice is off — have your child hunt the buzz with a hand on their own throat, then flip between the two sounds until the switch feels like theirs. V wants the buzz; that is the whole letter.'},
 'w-whale':{
  title:'Ww — Whale',one:'The gliding sound that starts rounded and slides open — anchored to the spouting giant of the sea.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Ww Lesson: Teach the W Sound with Whale (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Ww and its gliding /w/ sound, taught with the whale card — canvas tracing, a weather-reporter game and a W-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Ww and its glide with the whale card: your child traces the letter, delivers a full weather report in w-words, and learns the sound that moves — lips round, then slide open.',
  what:'Ww is the letter that stands for the first sound in whale — lips rounded small, then gliding open into the vowel with the voice on: wuh.',
  mean:'W is a moving sound: nothing stays still. The lips travel from a tight little "oo" shape out into the open word — a glide, not a stop. Sounds that move are actions as much as noises.',
  know:'Your child needs the round-then-open glide, a running voice, and a seaful of w-words: whale, water, wave, wind.',
  why:'/w/ powers water, want, we and wow — the splash words of childhood. It also looks like two v\u2019s but sounds like nothing else, which makes it a favourite sorting puzzle.',
  dev:'Lip-glide control (round to open), voice carry-through, and connecting a physical motion to a sound — w is phonics choreography.',
  vocab:'w, whale, water, wave, wind, wow',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Ww. Round your lips small, then open into the word — wuh! The whale sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Ww on the canvas: four waves, one glide each, voice running the whole way.\n3) Play the weather-reporter game below — the glide gets a whole forecast.\n4) Glide three w-things: whale, water, wave. Round, open, voice on.\n5) Finish with the arms-as-spout whoosh and one enormous WOW.',
  say:'"Pucker up like you are going to say oo — then slide open: wuh. That is w! W says wuh, like whale. Glide it: w, w, whale."',
  sayhow:'Lips rounded tightly, then released open with the voice running: "wuh". The motion is one smooth glide — if the teeth touch the lip, it turned into v; keep teeth and lip apart.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Ww sits beside a blue whale spouting water, and the word "whale" is printed below — the spout itself is the sound\u2019s shape: rounded, rising, opening out.',
  ask:'"Do your lips start round or open for w?" "Do they move or stay still?" "What w-word says itself in the bath?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "w-w-w-whale!" with rising arms, or report the news perfectly: "today is windy, wet and wavy" — a forecast with a phonics grade.',
  real:'Water play, windy walks and washing days are w-festivals. Waves at the beach (or in the bath), wobbly jelly, and every wow your child gasps — the glide is already in the day.',
  play:'Weather-reporter game: hand over a pretend microphone — your child is today\u2019s weather reporter. The forecast must be w-heavy and delivered with glides: "today is windy! expect waves! whales everywhere! wow!" Swap roles and let the grown-up flub a report on purpose ("today is very… d-windy?") so the child can correct the news.',
  check:'Ask for the sound and watch the lips: they should START rounded and MOVE open. If teeth bite the lip (vvvale), reset with "oo-uh" slid together fast. Then try r — w is round and gliding, r is curled and running.',
  tips:'If w bites into v, the teeth sneaked in — keep them behind the lip-line. The quick launch from a tiny lip-circle is the move to rehearse, and every weather report rehearses it for free.'},
 'x-xylophone':{
  title:'Xx — Xylophone',one:'The honest two-job letter — /ks/ at word endings, /z/ in this one famous word — anchored to the prettiest instrument.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Xx Lesson: Teach the X Sound with Xylophone (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Xx and its two jobs — /ks/ at the end of words and /z/ in xylophone — taught with the xylophone card, honestly.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Xx, the alphabet\u2019s famous oddball: mostly it says /ks/ at the END of words (box, fox, six), but on this card — at the start of xylophone — it borrows /z/. Both jobs are true, and children love the honesty.',
  what:'Xx has two jobs. Its day job is /ks/ at the end of words: box, fox, six, mix. Its rare gig is at the START of xylophone, where it sounds like /z/. This card shows the rare gig; the lesson teaches both, honestly.',
  mean:'X teaches the most grown-up phonics truth: not every letter follows the rules, and THAT IS FINE. Meeting the exception as a friend — “this one is the weird one!” — builds reading confidence, not confusion. Out loud, twice, until it feels like insider knowledge.',
  know:'Your child needs the /ks/ ending as x\u2019s main job, the /z/ exception named out loud as special, and the pride of knowing the alphabet\u2019s strangest letter.',
  why:'/ks/ finishes box, fox, six and mix — words a child already counts with. And xylophone gives x a famous face, so the rarest letter never feels forgotten.',
  dev:'Ending-sound attention (most lessons hunt beginnings; x hunts endings), honest exception-handling, and the flexibility that keeps rules and quirks friends.',
  vocab:'x, xylophone, box, fox, six, mix',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Xx — the weird one! At the end of words it says ks: box! But here, at the start of xylophone, it borrows z."\n2) Finger-trace the Xx on the canvas: two crossing strokes — one for /k/, one for /s/, crossing like the sounds do.\n3) Play the treasure-box game below; every lid close clicks /ks/.\n4) End-say three x-words: box, fox, six. Listen for the sound at the END.\n5) Finish with one rainbow xylophone glissando and the honorary title: x-insider.',
  say:'"X is a finisher — it loves the END of words: bo-ks! fo-ks! si-ks! Say them with me and listen to the ending. And xylophone? That is x\u2019s one fancy day job, sounding like z."',
  sayhow:'/ks/ is two sounds in a row: the back click (/k/) straight into the hiss (/s/) — "ks", quick and dry, at word endings. For xylophone, borrow the buzzing /z/ — and say honestly that this one is the exception.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Xx sits beside a rainbow toy xylophone with two mallets, and the word "xylophone" is printed below — the most beautiful exception in the whole alphabet.',
  ask:'"Where does x like to hide — the start or the end of words?" "What does x say in box?" "And in xylophone? (Careful — it is the weird one!)"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "box ends with ks!" while slamming lids, or guard the secret jealously: "xylophone starts with a z-sound — I KNOW, it is weird."',
  real:'Boxes close with a /ks/ click all day: lunch boxes, toy boxes, shoe boxes. Foxes fill storybooks, six fills counting, and xylophones wait in every music corner.',
  play:'Treasure-box game: fill an imaginary box with treasure. Each item must be named and boxed away with a proper /ks/ click: a fo-ks! a bo-ks! si-ks gold coins! When the lid slams shut — "KS!" — the treasure is safe. Then, for the finale, play the xylophone: x\u2019s one fancy, z-sounding day job, performed with two mallets and total seriousness.',
  check:'Ask where x lives: "at the END — box, fox, six" is the pass, plus the bonus fact that xylophone is the famous weird one. Two jobs named out loud, both honest — x is fully understood.',
  tips:'Teach the exception AS an exception — children adore being told "this one is the weird one", and the honesty makes them phonics insiders. Never pretend xylophone starts with /ks/; they will catch you.'},
 'y-yo-yo':{
  title:'Yy — Yo-Yo',one:'The launching glide — the sound that throws words into the air — anchored to the toy that bounces back.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Yy Lesson: Teach the Y Sound with Yo-Yo (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Yy and its gliding /y/ sound, taught with the yo-yo card — canvas tracing, a yawny game and a Y-sound hunt.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Yy and its launching glide with the yo-yo card: your child traces the letter, yawns through a y-word game, and learns the sound that throws yes, you and yo-yo into the air.',
  what:'Yy is the letter that stands for the first sound in yo-yo — the tongue high and forward, gliding straight into the vowel like a launch: yuh.',
  mean:'Y is a runway sound: it starts words the way a yo-yo leaves the hand — a quick slide that throws the word into the air. And y has a secret second life at word endings (happy, sky) saved for later.',
  know:'Your child needs the quick glide off the tongue, the launch feel, and a handful of y-words: yo-yo, yes, you, yell.',
  why:'/y/ launches yes, you, yawn and yummy — some of the most-used words in a small life. And the yawn game makes it the only letter sound a child can practice while genuinely falling asleep.',
  dev:'Front-glide control (the same runway as the vowels that follow), quick launch-and-release, and the social magic of yes and thank-you practice built in.',
  vocab:'y, yo-yo, yes, you, yell, yummy',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Yy. The tongue slides and launches the word — yuh! The yo-yo sound."\n2) Finger-trace the Yy on the canvas: one long arm and one short — the shape of a hand throwing a yo-yo down and catching it.\n3) Play the yawny game below; the glide arrives with the yawns.\n4) Launch three y-things: yo-yo, yes, yummy. One glide each, thrown into the room.\n5) Finish with a fist-up-fist-down yo-yo trick — yo, yo — and a real catch.',
  say:'"Slide your tongue up and let the word jump off: yuh. That is y! Y says yuh, like yo-yo. Launch it: y, y, yo-yo."',
  sayhow:'Tongue high and forward, then a quick glide into the vowel with the voice on: "yuh". One launch, no hold — y never waits, it throws.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Yy sits beside a blue yo-yo with a star and a long string, and the word "yo-yo" is printed below — the only toy whose name says itself twice, one y per syllable.',
  ask:'"Does y stay still or launch the word?" "What is the politest y-word?" "Can you say yo-yo without laughing?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "y-y-yo-yo!" with matching fist pumps, or use the lesson politically: "yes starts with y — I said the y-word!"',
  real:'Yo-yos, yoghurt and yawns; yellow things, yachts in books, and every yes and thank-you exchange of the day. The glide launches manners as often as toys.',
  play:'Yawny game: y is the letter of yawns — so yawn. Each giant pretend yawn must land on a y-word: yaaaawn… yo-yo! yaaaawn… yes! yaaaawn… yummy! The bigger the yawn, the further the word flies. Bedtime bonus: this is the only phonics game that ends in an actual nap.',
  check:'Ask for the sound: a quick gliding "yuh" is the pass. Then say yo-yo syllable by syllable — two yaws, two y-sounds, one word. If the glide launches cleanly, y\u2019s first job is owned (the vowel job at word endings waits happily for later).',
  tips:'If y comes out heavy as "juh", lighten it — the tongue slides, it does not jump (that is j\u2019s move). The oo-yes trick helps: say "ee" then open fast — ee-yes becomes yes, and the glide is discovered.'},
 'z-zebra':{
  title:'Zz — Zebra',one:'The buzzing hiss — s\u2019s loud twin — anchoring the grand finale of the alphabet.',
  seoTitle:'Letter Zz Lesson: Teach the Z Sound with Zebra (Class 28, Ages 4–5)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Learning Path phonics lesson for ages 4–5: the letter Zz and its buzzing /z/ sound, taught with the zebra card — canvas tracing, a zip-up game and the A–Z sound-wall finale.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches Zz, the last letter on the path: the s-hiss with the voice switched on. Your child traces the letter, zips through a z-word parade, and closes the whole alphabet with the loudest coat in the set.',
  what:'Zz is the letter that stands for the first sound in zebra — the snake hiss of s with the voice buzzing: zzz. Same teeth, same tongue, one switch turned on.',
  mean:'Z is the alphabet\u2019s finish line, learned as a switch: find the hiss, turn on the buzz. Children who just learned s can borrow its whole mouth-shape — z is s wearing a bee costume.',
  know:'Your child needs the buzzing hiss, the s-to-z switch as a party trick, and a parade of z-words: zebra, zoo, zip, zero.',
  why:'/z/ carries zip, zoo, zoom and zero — the words of speed and nothing. It closes the alphabet with a built-in celebration: the sound-wall finale, where all twenty-six sounds line up for one last parade.',
  dev:'Voice-switch mastery (the s/z pair completes the twin collection), buzz control on a familiar shape, and the memory-and-recall feat of the A–Z sound wall.',
  vocab:'z, zebra, zoo, zip, zero, zoom',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Zz. Hiss like s — but buzz! zzz. The zebra sound, and the LAST letter!"\n2) Finger-trace the Zz on the canvas: across, diagonal, across — one buzz per stroke.\n3) Play the zip-up game below; the sound lives in every jacket.\n4) Buzz three z-things: zebra, zoo, zip. Sss with the switch on.\n5) Finish with the sound-wall finale: all the letter sounds from A to Z, ending on the loudest zebra cheer.',
  say:'"Hiss like a snake — now turn your voice on and feel it buzz: zzz. That is z! Z says zzz, like zebra. The last letter, with the loudest coat."',
  sayhow:'Teeth nearly closed like s, air through, voice ON: "zzz". It hums while it hisses. If z comes out as s, the throat went quiet — hand on the throat, switch the bee back on.',
  notice:'On this card the big navy pair Zz sits beside a black-and-white striped zebra, and the word "zebra" is printed below — the alphabet\u2019s boldest coat, on the very last letter of the path.',
  ask:'"Is z buzzing or quiet?" "What is the difference between sun and zoo?" "What z-word zips?"',
  childSay:'Your child might say: "zzz-zebra!" like a bee landing, or announce the graduation moment: "I did the WHOLE alphabet — A to zzz!"',
  real:'Zips on jackets, bags and tents buzz all day. Zoo trips, zooming cars and zero biscuits left close the set\u2019s vocabulary — and bedtime bees provide the buzz on demand.',
  play:'Zip-up game: hand over a real jacket (or mime one). Zip it slowly while buzzing — "zzz-zz-zz-IP!" — and each successful zip earns a z-word: zip, zoo, zebra, zero. Unzip, buzz backwards, zip again. Then the finale: the whole alphabet\u2019s sounds, paraded from A to Z — the zebra always closes the show and always gets the biggest cheer.',
  check:'Ask for the sound and listen for the buzz inside the hiss. Then the grand test: sun (quiet hiss) and zoo (buzzing hiss) back to back — if your child can switch the voice on and off, the twin collection is complete, and so is the alphabet.',
  tips:'Many children say z like s at first — the mouth shape is right, the switch is just off. Hand on the throat, buzz it on, and end every session with the A–Z sound wall: nothing finishes the alphabet like the zebra\u2019s cheer.'}
};
